from research import *
items=[
('Barát / Thomassen','cycles','Problem 1.1','What relaxation of the red induced-subgraph conditions ensures a crumby-style blue/red coloring of every 3-connected cubic graph? Blue maximum degree stays at most one.','Exclude as an insufficiently specified endpoint: allowing arbitrary red paths trivializes a literal relaxation. Original conjecture is already false in the source; do not count another relaxation.'),
('Cambie','cycles','Problem 2.2','For every finite graph G, is its list packing number at most twice its list chromatic number?','Hold: a universal list-assignment problem, not reducible to one ordinary coloring witness. Nearby 2.1 and 2.3–2.11 are not padded as extra families.'),
('Horák–Rosa, presented by Hahn','cycles','Conjecture 3.1','For every k>=1, n=4k+1 and k-element subset S of {1,...,2k}, XP(2n,S) is (2k+1)-edge-colorable except the Petersen graph. Layer 1 uses S, layer 2 its complement, with a perfect matching joining corresponding vertices.','Hold: source attributes the question to 2005. Concrete certificate possible, but original 2005 body and closest successors not acquired; cannot certify an unresolved target.'),
('Hahn','cycles','Problem 4.1','Does an absolute c>=0 guarantee rc(G)=2 for every noncomplete n-vertex graph with delta(G)>=n/2+c? In particular, does c=0 work?','Hold: inspected 2609.11437v1 Question 1.1, Theorems 1.9/1.11 and Lemma 2.1. Full finite statement survives those theorems; logarithmic surplus or o(n^2) exceptional pairs does not answer it. Famous broad endpoint, no new solving mechanism here.'),
('Mattiolo / Ma–Mattiolo–Steffen–Wolf','cycles','Problem 5.2','Does there exist a 5-edge-connected 5-graph in which every pair of perfect matchings intersects? A 5-graph is 5-regular with every odd vertex cut of size at least 5.','Hold: direct finite witness route but source connects nonexistence to Fan–Raspaud. Original 2023 proof body not inspected; no constructive family found. Do not split edge-colorability and matching clauses into credits.'),
('Schiermeyer','cycles','Problem 6.1','Describe all triangle-free graphs of maximum degree at most 3 and chromatic index 4.','Park: broad classification contains all snarks; source gives edge subdivision of a triangle-free 3-edge-colorable cubic graph as a known class. Reproducing this class would not answer the question.'),
('Caro–Tuza','cycles','Problem 7.1','Decide in deterministic polynomial time whether an arbitrary finite simple graph admits Type A (earlier degree zero or odd at every vertex) or Type B (also permits an even earlier degree when later degree is zero); construct an order when one exists.','Rank 1 provisional: investigate Type A source-set compression. Problems 7.1 and 7.2 treated as one recognition/search family, with the literal Type B clause retained as an additional residual obligation.'),
('Stembridge, presented by Brenti','coxeter','Problem 1.1','For every Coxeter system (W,S), is sum over reflections t in T of x^length(t) rational?','Hold: ordinary group growth rationality in the preceding paragraph does not imply rationality of the reflection sublanguage. No finite-state mechanism or closest-successor body acquired.'),
('Karp','polynomials','Open problem. Find sufficient conditions on a1','For positive real a1,...,ad,b and positive integers m1,...,md, find sufficient conditions for the numerator A(x) in the displayed Miller–Paris transformed hypergeometric generating function to have only real (negative) zeros.','Park: asks for sufficient conditions, while already naming the d-Narayana class. A new parameter instance or routine multiplier bridge would not resolve a uniquely specified full endpoint. Distinct from Toeplitz stability; that neighboring question was not selected.'),
('Saff','polynomials','Equilibrium open problem for multi-dimensional annulus','For d-2<s<d, determine the Riesz s-energy minimizing probability measure supported on {x in R^d:r<=|x|<=R}.','Hold: nonroutine analytic endpoint. Source quotes Riesz ball solution and cites 2108.00534 Appendix D.2; ball formula does not settle positive inner radius. No full inversion/positivity mechanism derived.'),
('Nikolov','polynomials','Conjecture (G.N.)','For every nonnegative continuous even convex majorant on [-1,1], do its snake polynomials have nonnegative first-kind Chebyshev coefficients in the source orientation?','Exclude: exact semantic duplicate. Inspected cached primary proof body SnakeChebyshevExpansionRefutation.lean and problem explanation: degree-six explicit majorant, alternation and negative constant coefficient -459/274. No new investigation invested after match.'),
('Shapiro','polynomials','Conjecture 1. For any polynomial P','For every polynomial of degree at least three whose roots are in convex position but not a regular polygon, the closure of zeros of all derivatives of all positive powers is a closed domain; all critical points are on its boundary; that boundary has no inflections and lies in the critical-value envelope of z-alpha P/P-prime, alpha in [0,d].','Hold: all four clauses are one family, not four scouts. Fixed-power derivative zeros alone cannot certify the closure boundary. No asymptotic boundary proof acquired.'),
('Bille–Buchstaber–Spodarev','fullerenes','Conjecture 3. For','For every alpha,beta in (0,1) and every pair of feasible-size dual fullerenes, equality of trace exp(alpha A+beta D) is equivalent to graph isomorphism.','Hold: concrete spectral collision or parameter crossing could refute this universal assertion. Same n is required for a cospectral pair; equality of adjacency spectra alone is insufficient because D is nonconstant on dual fullerenes. No certified pair obtained.'),
('Bille–Buchstaber–Spodarev','fullerenes','How can one construct a uniform distribution','Construct a uniform random isomorphism class among all n-vertex fullerenes for each feasible even n>=20, n!=22.','Exclude literal existence endpoint: Section 3.1 already supplies enumeration followed by a uniform integer. Efficiency is the substantive motivation but no exact complexity endpoint is specified. Do not claim solving the question by repeating Section 3.1.'),
('Gupur / Linton','reliability','When g(x) and f(x) are non-constants','Establish well-posedness and time-asymptotic behavior of the two-unit parallel redundant model displayed in Section 4, for nonconstant repair/failure rates g(x),f(x), with the stated transport equations, renewal boundaries and Dirac initial data.','Park: original model attributed to Linton 1976; rate hypotheses are not made precise enough at the question for an all-rates theorem. Constant-rate solution is explicitly covered. Do not invent coercivity assumptions and call them a full resolution.')]
rows=[]
for i,(author,key,label,scope,status) in enumerate(items,1):
 textbody=(L/'primary'/(key+'.txt')).read_text();pos=textbody.find(label)
 if key=='reliability':pos=textbody.find('In 1976, Linton')
 if pos<0:raise RuntimeError(label)
 page=textbody[:pos].count('\f')+1
 rows.append(dict(id=i,author=author,source=key+'.pdf',page=page,label=label,statement=scope,disposition=status))
(L/'candidates.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows,indent=2))
s='# Primary-first scouting\n\nFifteen distinct families; candidate statements generated from the frozen primary bodies, then screened against the immutable cached public registry. Search pages were navigation, not evidence of resolution. Adjacent clauses are grouped. This pass ranges over many authors and topics, but the acquired problem-posing collections are concentrated in 2023–2024; 1976 and 2005 are historical attributions, not a claim to have read those originals. That is a remaining breadth limitation.\n\n'
for r in rows:s+=f"## {r['id']}. {r['author']}\n\n{r['source']}, PDF page {r['page']}, {r['label']}.\n\n{r['statement']}\n\n{r['disposition']}\n\n"
s+='## Ranking and eligibility\n\nOnly one provisional actionable survivor is ranked: Caro–Tuza Type A recognition, workshop v1 PDF p10. The older original exposition is 2408.04257v1, Definitions 3.10–3.11 and Problems 6.12–6.13, PDF pp23–24 and 52. Its apparent permutation/index convention is read through the unambiguous workshop earlier/later-neighbor definition. Nearest inspected theorem: Király–Kisfaludi-Bak 1410.1653v1 Proposition 9, PDF p3, reducing prescribed parity acyclic orientations to dual-criticality. The new calculation is the independent-source-set reduction in discovery.md. Strongest eligibility objection: it is only an exponential reduction; randomized recognition of each fixed parity case does not give deterministic polynomial recognition over all source sets. Type B is also unhandled. No rank is awarded merely for absence of a cache hit.\n'
(L/'SCOUTING.md').write_text(s)
print([(r['id'],r['source'],r['page']) for r in rows])
